<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottoman Public Debt Administration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ottoman Public Debt Administration was an organisation established in 1881 after the Ottoman state defaulted on its foreign loans. Run by representatives of European creditors, it collected certain state revenues directly to service the debt. It placed much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>'s finances under foreign supervision, a marker of its loss of fiscal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Administration was set up by a treaty that defined its powers, and the British public, angered by the default, lobbied for its creation. The Ottoman state handed it monopolies on salt and tobacco and the taxes on stamps, alcohol, fishing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> in certain regions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> among them, together with taxes on shops and customs duties. If these fell short, it could also claim a share of the indemnities owed by Balkan states under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-berlin-1889">Treaty of Berlin</a>, which had itself shrunk the empire's territory, population and tax base.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its governing council had one representative each of the British, French, German, Austrian, Italian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Ottoman creditors and one of the Ottoman state. It became a large, essentially independent bureaucracy inside the Ottoman one, with 5,000 officials collecting taxes for the creditors and 9,000 employees at its peak, more than the empire's finance ministry. Ottoman Muslims made up roughly 88 to 94 per cent of its staff and drew stable salaries, but Europeans and Ottoman Christians held most senior posts, and during the crises of 1895-1899 and 1907-1908 it took on European and non-Muslim staff out of all proportion, out of distrust of Muslim employees.</p><p>Uncertain accounts and a gap in knowledge between the Administration and the Ottoman government made the true size of the debt hard to fix, and the difficulty advanced statistics and accounting; British bondholders may have been favoured in the repayments. The Administration also acted as an intermediary for European companies seeking investments, and by 1900 it was financing many railways and other industrial projects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The historian Donald Quataert argues that its discriminatory hiring sharpened tensions between the empire's religious communities and helped wear down its social fabric. After the empire's dissolution, Ottoman revenue stamps overprinted O.P.D.A. or A.D.P.O., or printed with those letters, were issued between 1918 and 1924 for use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, Transjordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottoman Public Debt Administration” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Capitulations, treaty arrangements granting European nationals exemption from Ottoman jurisdiction and favourable tariffs, constrained the empire’s fiscal sovereignty, and the <b>Ottoman Public Debt Administration</b> established in 1881 placed much of its revenue under European creditor control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Borrowing for infrastructure, some £200 million from British and French banks by 1875, ended in bankruptcy that year, and in 1881 the European powers set up the <b>Ottoman Public Debt Administration</b> to control revenues such as the tobacco taxes.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
